==============================================================================
F1  DISCRIMINATION -- dimension is a free parameter, not a product
==============================================================================

  Kronecker's code_dim is forced: 256 byte values x 32 positions = 8192. Convolution
  binding has no such product, so d can be chosen. The question is how small it can go
  before two tokens share a code.
  d=   64  collisions=    0  proj@D=8096 =    0.5M
  d=  128  collisions=    0  proj@D=8096 =    1.0M
  d=  256  collisions=    0  proj@D=8096 =    2.1M
  d=  512  collisions=    0  proj@D=8096 =    4.1M
  d= 1024  collisions=    0  proj@D=8096 =    8.3M

  shipped kronecker onehot d_p=32: code_dim 8192, 336 tokens lost, 66.3M params
  smallest zero-collision dimension: d=64  (128x smaller than shipped)

==============================================================================
F2  SEPARATION MARGIN -- and where extra dimensions stop helping
==============================================================================
  d=  128  max adversarial cosine = 0.98326
  d=  256  max adversarial cosine = 0.97792
  d=  512  max adversarial cosine = 0.97698
  d= 1024  max adversarial cosine = 0.97686
  d= 2048  max adversarial cosine = 0.97390

  The margin is essentially flat in d. These pairs are near-identical strings that
  differ only in their last byte or two, so their superpositions genuinely overlap;
  extra dimensions add room but not distance. This is the honest weak spot of the
  approach relative to a tuned dynamic Kronecker codec, which reaches 0.964.

==============================================================================
F3  LEARNABILITY -- the test the lossiness could fail
==============================================================================

  Same rig as the problem-3 submission, so the arms are comparable. Every token carries
  a deterministic label and inside each shipped-collision group the labels disagree, so
  a codec that merges them is capped at the majority share. Kronecker's code is exact
  and sparse; this one superposes waves with crosstalk, which is exactly the property
  that might not survive contact with a trained model.

  336 tokens, 155 collision groups
  ceiling for a codec that collides them: 52.1%
  kronecker onehot d_p=32    acc= 52.1%  loss=0.6861  input-path params=524,288
  fourier d=256              acc=100.0%  loss=0.0000  input-path params=16,384
  fourier d=2048             acc=100.0%  loss=0.0016  input-path params=131,072
  dense table (control)      acc=100.0%  loss=0.0000  input-path params=21,504

==============================================================================
F4  INVERTIBILITY -- reading the byte string back out of the code
==============================================================================

  Unbinding is what makes this a real Fourier scheme rather than a re-parameterisation.
  Correlating the code against a candidate (byte, position) wave recovers whether that
  pair is present, so the whole token can be decoded by argmax over 256 candidates per
  position. Nothing is stored per token and no table is consulted.
  d=  128  whole-token decode  23.0%   byte accuracy  57.1%
  d=  256  whole-token decode  54.5%   byte accuracy  83.8%
  d=  512  whole-token decode  86.8%   byte accuracy  97.9%
  d= 1024  whole-token decode  99.8%   byte accuracy 100.0%
  d= 2048  whole-token decode 100.0%   byte accuracy 100.0%

  byte accuracy by token byte-length (d=1024):
     0- 7 bytes: 100.0%  (410 bytes)
     8-15 bytes: 100.0%  (2344 bytes)
    16-23 bytes: 100.0%  (1239 bytes)
    24-31 bytes: 100.0%  (1125 bytes)
    32-39 bytes:  99.7%  (303 bytes)

  nearest-code decode (vocabulary-bounded: match against known codes)
    d=   64  correct token retrieved 100.00%
    d=  128  correct token retrieved 100.00%
    d=  256  correct token retrieved 100.00%
    d= 1024  correct token retrieved 100.00%

  Two regimes, and they cost very differently. Byte-argmax decoding is vocabulary-free
  -- it can emit strings the model never saw -- and needs d ~ 20*L. Nearest-code
  decoding is bounded by the vocabulary but works wherever discrimination works, which
  F1 showed is d=64. The paper's Hypothesis A wants the first; a cheap tied head only
  needs the second.

==============================================================================
F5  POSITION RAMP -- random phases vs the canonical circular shift
==============================================================================

  ramp="shift" makes binding an exact circular shift (the textbook shift theorem), which
  is more interpretable. But a circular shift wraps: position p and position p+d are the
  same phase, so the window reappears -- just at d instead of 32. ramp="random" has no
  period. This is the same stored-vs-computed trade the course keeps meeting.
  ramp=random   |<phase(p=0), phase(p=d)>| = 0.0530   <- no period
  ramp=shift    |<phase(p=0), phase(p=d)>| = 1.0000   <- wraps, window at d

